According to the documentation, `fetch` has no built-in timeout option in React Native. Instead, you should use an `AbortController` and call `abort()` from a `setTimeout` [networking#2].

Here's the approach:

```javascript
const controller = new AbortController();
const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000); // 5 second timeout

try {
  const response = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
  // handle response
} catch (error) {
  if (error.name === 'AbortError') {
    // timeout occurred
  }
} finally {
  clearTimeout(timeoutId);
}
```

This allows you to abort the fetch request if it doesn't complete within your specified timeout period.

