To set a timeout on `fetch` in React Native, you should use an `AbortController` and call `abort()` from a `setTimeout` [networking#6].

Here's the approach:

```javascript
const controller = new AbortController();
const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000); // 5 second timeout

try {
  const response = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
  clearTimeout(timeoutId);
  // handle response
} catch (error) {
  // handles timeout and other network errors
}
```

This works because `fetch` has no built-in timeout option [networking#6], so you need to manually implement it using `AbortController`.
